<urn:usf:delivery-facet:universalservicefoundationscopeandprinciplespermissions> <urn:usf:predicate:facetStatement> "As a shared platform-governance asset it owns no tenant-scoped records and exposes no tenant-mutating routes, so standard platform security controls apply rather than per-tenant authorization. Authority over changes flows through the USF constitutional and authority order (semantic definitions, ADRs, validators, proof, then source), not through end-user roles. Its effect on tenants is indirect: it constrains shared platform behaviour without owning or exposing tenant data." <urn:usf:graph:v3-delivery> .
