<urn:usf:mechanism-assessment-item:capture-event-vocabulary> <urn:usf:predicate:classification> "PARTIALLY_SUPERSEDED_V1_CAPTURE_MECHANISM" <urn:usf:graph:v3-delivery> .
<urn:usf:mechanism-assessment-item:capture-event-vocabulary> <urn:usf:predicate:invariantProtected> "Each product event carries a stated delivery guarantee and a declared producing capability, over a closed product-event vocabulary." <urn:usf:graph:v3-delivery> .
<urn:usf:mechanism-assessment-item:capture-event-vocabulary> <urn:usf:predicate:legacyTrustBoundary> "None. V2 carried the vocabulary forward and grew it to 60 events, so this is a discontinuity at the V1-to-V3 jump rather than a deliberate deprecation." <urn:usf:graph:v3-delivery> .
<urn:usf:mechanism-assessment-item:capture-event-vocabulary> <urn:usf:predicate:reason> "Two things were carried only by the domain-event class and have no V3 counterpart. First, a per-event delivery guarantee: all 50 V1 events declared at-least-once, and V3 declares no delivery-guarantee predicate anywhere, so the guarantee survives as prose inside the event-bus facets and as an idempotency key rather than as a property a new event must state. Second, a closed product-event vocabulary bound to its producer: V3 types an event as a free-form string and has no producesEvent edge, so the fact that one capability publishes exactly one named set is unrepresentable. The graph names canonical product events in facet prose without declaring them, which is the clearest evidence of the gap. auditmodel cannot express either: it records that something happened, not how its delivery is guaranteed." <urn:usf:graph:v3-delivery> .
<urn:usf:mechanism-assessment-item:capture-event-vocabulary> <urn:usf:predicate:type> <urn:usf:type:MechanismAssessmentItem> <urn:usf:graph:v3-delivery> .
<urn:usf:mechanism-assessment-item:capture-event-vocabulary> <urn:usf:predicate:v3Mechanism> "The occurrence-recording half is superseded and satisfied. V1 modelled the same occurrence twice, once as a bus-delivered domain event and once as an audit record: 45 of its 50 domain-event canonical names also exist as audit-event names. V3 carries that half under the auditmodel facet kind, with 52 exported audit-event-kind constants yielding 147 distinct kinds across the realised capability modules, 67 auditmodel facetStatements, and audit assertions in every one of the 64 conformance suites. The delivery-contract half has no V3 mechanism at all." <urn:usf:graph:v3-delivery> .
